# [M6.4.3] D27 blinded corpus -- atomic-groups module.
#
# docs/spec/match_api.md S3.1's find-all loop: report a match, then resume
# at its END if non-empty or at the next character boundary past its START
# if empty; `<prefix>_search`'s offsets are always ABSOLUTE into the
# subject, never relative to startpos. The .rxt format has no native
# repeat-loop primitive, so this file hand-simulates the successive steps
# such a loop would take with `ms`/`ns` at the startpos values the loop
# itself would visit, checked against libpcre2's own startoffset-driven
# `pcre2_match` (which is exactly what `<prefix>_search`'s startpos
# threads, per that section) -- not against a hand-rolled "what the loop
# should do" guess.
#
# Every block carries `features atomic-groups`, per atomic_basic.rxt.

# (?>a?) is nullable -- an atomic group can and does produce an empty
# match, and does so at the very end of the subject once every non-empty
# option is exhausted. Simulated loop over "aa": step 1 (p=0) finds a
# non-empty match and would resume at its end (1); step 2 (p=1) finds
# another non-empty match and would resume at its end (2); step 3 (p=2)
# finds the EMPTY match at the end -- the case the loop's special
# advance rule exists for.
pattern (?>a?)
features atomic-groups
m "aa" 0 1
ms 1 "aa" 1 2
ms 2 "aa" 2 2

# The absolute-offset promise: searching from a nonzero startpos still
# reports the match's true position in the subject, not an offset
# relative to where the search began.
pattern (?>a+)b
features atomic-groups
ms 1 "xaaab" 1 5

# The atomic trap persists at a nonzero startpos exactly as it does at 0
# -- it is a property of the pattern at whatever position it is tried
# from, not specific to position 0.
pattern (?>a+)a
features atomic-groups
ns 1 "xaaab"

# A possessive optional GROUP under the find-all loop: two non-empty
# matches, then an empty match at the very end -- same empty-match-
# advance shape as the (?>a?) case above, now through the possessive
# spelling instead of (?>...).
pattern (?:ab)?+
features atomic-groups
m "abab" 0 2
ms 2 "abab" 2 4
ms 4 "abab" 4 4

# A possessive bounded repeat, searched from two different non-adjacent
# startpos values a real loop would use after a prior non-empty match --
# the two windows do not overlap and each reports its own absolute span.
pattern a{1,2}+
features atomic-groups
ms 1 "xaaaa" 1 3
ms 3 "xaaaa" 3 5
